# 📓️ Build system field guide: one workspace, many agents, 16 GB

Reference for people and agents who build in the same checkout at the same time. English first, Deutsch below. The contracts, measurements and history are in [README.md](README.md) (Build tools guide); every command and variable here exists in the code. Status words: **live** = in the tree, **pending gate** = staged or just applied and waiting for the landing gate (`result-gate-g.md` lists the order and the product build after each batch), **in progress** = a lane has not finished it, **not verified** = built and tested graph-free only.

## 🚀️ Run a build

```bash
bun repo run <project>:<target>                    # one task and its closure
bun repo run-many --targets=build,test --projects=a,b
bun repo affected --targets=test                   # changed against HEAD and dependents
bun repo run <project>:<target> --tool=bazel       # --tool=nx|bazel, else SEMIO_BUILD_TOOL, else the policy default (nx)
bun repo run <project>:<target> --dry-run          # plan only; --skip-cache ignores the task cache once
SEMIO_BUILD_GRAPH_POLICY=current bun repo run …    # serve the newest sealed graph, never wait for a rebuild
bun repo show projects --graph-policy=current      # read-only queries: same switch as a flag
SEMIO_REPO_PROFILE=1 bun repo run …                # phase table of every process (json for JSON)
bun repo doctor [--only=leases,capacity]           # why is work waiting, slow or failing, with the fixing command
```

- Everything goes through `repo`; never start `nx`, `bazel`, `cargo`, `rustc` or a private `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`. Nested tasks inherit `SEMIO_BUILD_TOOL`.
- Through MCP the tools `run`, `test`, `run_many`, `affected` and the rest map one to one and take `agent`. `watch`, `reset`, `cache gc|clear|dedupe` are not MCP tools (human decisions).
- Dashboard, architecture course and print are green on Bazel after each of the gate batches 1-4 (course, dashboard, print, course; `result-gate-g.md`); the course e2e passed 76/76 on both topologies (coordination 07:30).

## 🔬️ What happens on a run

1. **Door** (35-75 MB): plans the route, then waits in the **capacity queue** alone (no loader child while it waits). A run that needs the graph and only waits for a peer's graph build also waits as the door alone: after 15 s in the queue it yields (exit 75 internally), waits for a newer generation or 10 min, and restarts with its first request time, at most 3 times.
2. **Graph**: one builder holds `repository-graph-facts:<root>:shard:7`. Every build stamps the pointer with the time it began observing and the owners it verified (coverage stamp); a waiter that began earlier is served at once (`graph: generation N was sealed by a peer after this command began and already covers it`). Stale owners are first inferred in claimed chunks of at most 128 owners by any number of processes (`graph: … prepared/preparing`; compute slots follow RAM and the learned size of a rebuild, `SEMIO_GRAPH_COMPUTERS=<n>` overrides), then one exclusive assembly publishes. Inference memory is governed (a quarter of the host's RAM and half of the run's grant, clamped to 1-16 GB; `SEMIO_GRAPH_MEMORY_MB` overrides). A project whose source does not parse, or whose dependency is not in the snapshot, is **withheld** instead of failing the generation. `SEMIO_GRAPH_PHASES=1` prints phase timings.
   - **Graph memo** (live, `SEMIO_BUILD_GRAPH_MEMO=on|off`, default on, any other value is an error): a repeated identical request is answered without starting the graph child. The first run of a request keeps a light record. The next run lets its graph child classify every fact of the generation it serves (what the fact reads: the entry it names, the paths inside its key, a resolved request) and seals the record. From the third run on, when the change journal proves that no directory the planned slice read has changed since the record's cursor and the facts outside the journal's sight (symbolic-link targets, `node_modules`, `.cache`, bare module requests) still hold by the engine's own check, the stored slice is served and the run prints `graph: 198 projects served from the memo of 12 s ago; the journal vouches for 218205 facts and 288 facts outside its sight held, no planner process was started`; the planner child (5-7 s of every hot run) never starts. A hot course no-op takes 10 s at 1.1-1.6 GB this way. The record covers the slice the request plans (the owners of its closure and the shared environment), not the whole generation, so a project outside the slice whose dependency link moved cannot refuse it. The key holds the root, the arguments, the tool, the build mode, the identity of the running implementation and the `SEMIO_BUILD_`, `SEMIO_GRAPH_` and `SEMIO_REPO_` variables minus the per-run ones (granted job count `--parallel=N`, graph yield handover, generation pin, `SEMIO_REPO_PROFILE`); the first run after an edit of the build tool therefore starts a new record. Records (at most 8) live in the private runtime directory of the change journal, per user and checkout, never in the checkout, and go with the journal's epoch. A refusal is always the safe answer: the graph is planned as before and the run prints `graph: the memo cannot serve this request (<reason>); the graph is planned` (reasons in "When it goes wrong"; a request without any record prints nothing). `SEMIO_REPO_PROFILE=1` marks `graph.memo` as `hit`, `miss:<reason>` or `off`, and `graph.memo-seal` as `sealed`, `light` or the reason it could not seal. On macOS the file watcher ignores events that only report a copy-on-write clone, so creating a mirror of the tree no longer ends the vouch.
   - **Graph memo, unread siblings** (live since the gate group G34): per directory the slice only lists, the memo keeps the entry stamp (mtime, inode) the graph recorded. A row that adds, removes or renames a name directly inside such a directory is relevant; a row that only says `modified` is settled by one `lstat` against the stamp. An in-place edit of a sibling the slice does not read (append, truncate and write, `touch`) leaves the stamp alone and the memo is still served (`rechecked` counts one more fact per touched directory); a creation, deletion, rename, atomic save (temporary file and rename) or lock file moves the stamp and refuses with `changed: <path>`. Directories that a resolution or a derived fact names, and directories without a usable stamp (not in the frontier, or touched within 2 s of the capture), keep the strict rule: any row directly inside refuses. The first run after the landing misses once (the sealed format changed) and the two runs after it seal again.
3. **Nested pin**: a `repo run` started inside an orchestrated Bazel or semio action reads the generation of its parent (`SEMIO_BUILD_GENERATION_PIN`) and inherits its capacity grant, so it never waits for the graph or the machine behind itself.
   - **Graph memo, across generations, edits and derived facts** (live, gate groups G36-G43): (1) a sealed slice is served although other products published newer generations in between, when its own projects and the shared environment are unchanged there (`graph: the memo of generation 628 still describes generation 629: the 550 projects it serves are unchanged there`); (2) the child that seals a record also seals when a newer generation holds the identical slice, so a build of another product between the first and the second run of a request no longer keeps it `light`; (3) when the journal reports changed paths below the slice the memo no longer refuses at once: it re-checks only the facts that read those paths with the planner's own checks (an analysis is replayed and compared by its result) and serves when they all hold (`graph: the memo re-checked the 2 facts that read the 1 changed paths with the planner's own checks; they all still hold`). An in-place edit or an atomic save that keeps the imports and exports of a file is served (print one-line edit: no planner child, 85.5 s, the revert 15 s); an added import, a renamed or deleted analysed file, a touched path that became a symbolic link, more than 256 paths or 4096 rows, or a fact kind the check cannot verify still ends in `changed: <path>` and the planner; (4) stale rows that only derived TypeScript-program facts depend on (an edit of a course source) are asked again by a child of the same invocation, `build:graph-derive`, with the planner's own derivation, at most 64 questions (`graph: 2 derived facts that read them were asked again by a child process with the planner's own derivation; every answer is unchanged`); it serves only when every answer is equal and its new inputs were read before, else the graph is planned (the first product proof, gate run g43, was pending when this was written); (5) the listing of a task's own output directory (`.🧾️store`) is re-checked by the engine instead of by the journal, so the lock, temporary and rename rows of a build's own store writes no longer refuse the next run. A record is never sealed from a verification older than the journal cursor of its request.
4. **Admission** (`repo capacity status`):
   - **Gate**: a run is admitted while free memory stays above the floor (12.5 % of RAM clamped to 1536-4096 MB: 2048 MB on 16 GB, 4096 MB on 32 GB), swap stays below 25 %, load below 2 per core and pressure is not critical; fair queue, aging 5 min, at most 6 overtakes.
   - **Size**: a run reserves its operation default (`show`/`graph` 5120 MB, `dependencies` 6144 MB, tasks 768-4096 MB per kind) scaled by what earlier runs of its class really peaked (decayed 0.9 quantile of the last 16, +25 % margin, 30-200 % of the default, at most half of the memory above the floor; `[capacity] run: sized at 43% of the default from 5 earlier run(s) of its class`). What a run peaked at is its memory FOOTPRINT (macOS `phys_footprint`, Linux proportional set size plus swapped, Windows private working set), not its resident set, which on macOS stays at the high-water mark after memory was freed; the sample falls back to the resident set once, in both languages, where the footprint cannot be read. The state is `estimates.json`, stamped `measure: footprint` (a store of another measure is empty and rewritten), in the host-capacity cache directory (`~/Library/Caches/semio/host-capacity`, `$XDG_CACHE_HOME` or `~/.cache`, `%LOCALAPPDATA%`). A reservation counts in full until the class normally peaks (60 s up to 30 min, 5 min for an unknown class); at most 2 shrunk reservations ramp at once. A failed run never lowers an estimate.
   - **Phases** (live, gate batch 4): a run marks assembly, plan, rebuild and execution (graph load, graph build, tool start). The graph build learns its own class `phase:rebuild` and never inflates the class of the task; the reservation is the highest of the phases still to come and shrinks at each boundary (`… at least 1125 MB because of its plan phase`). The compute slots of a rebuild follow what a rebuild measurably takes.
   - **Quiet grant** (live, gate batch 4b): a run of a Bazel-gated tool whose class mostly executed nothing holds only a small reservation (`[capacity] run: forecast quiet from 5 earlier run(s) of its class (100% of them executed nothing): 1500 MB reserved now, the full 5632 MB only if work has to run`), passes the queue barrier (at most 4 quiet grants) and grows into the full request in the fair queue with the position it arrived with, once the planner, a graph rebuild or the first Bazel action that really runs needs it (`… work has to run, waiting 12s to grow the reservation from 1500 MB to 5632 MB`). A run that executed anything is learned as busy.
   - **Quiet follow-up** (live): the run that follows a finished run of its class within 30 minutes is forecast quiet even when the class has little quiet history, because the finished run left its results cached (`[capacity] run: forecast quiet because the earlier run of its class finished 90s ago and left its results cached: …`); a quiet grant ignores the soft stops (raised memory pressure, load, open files, attached images) and the hard stops (critical pressure, swap, memory) still hold it. What a quiet run may grow into is bounded (next bullets), so a hot no-op never waits for a size no run of its class ever used: a hot course no-op holds the 2048 MB quiet reservation and finishes in 10 s at 1.1-1.6 GB.
   - **Envelopes**: the policy declares what a multi-worker target holds (`end-to-end`: 4 cores, 6144 MB for targets named `test-e2e…`), so the admitted job carries it and the end-to-end stack planner counts on it.
   - **Declared machine**: `SEMIO_HOST_CAPACITY_TOTAL_MEGABYTES` (e.g. `16384`) and `SEMIO_HOST_CAPACITY_CORES` (e.g. `8`) make a larger host plan as a smaller one in the gate, the worker policy, the Bazel server plan and the test runners; a declaration only shrinks, a malformed value means the real host, and the shared sample file is reused only by processes declaring the same machine.
   - **Full-request bound**: the full request a quiet run grows into (and every admission) is bounded per class: by the peak the class measured itself (90th percentile of its own last runs plus 25%, once 3 runs kept their peaks; the pooled peaks of other products never count), else by its declared ceiling (`classCeilings`, the course build 12288 MB), else by `estimates.fallbackCeilingMegabytes` (8192 MB) - never by 200% of the default (a dashboard build that really peaks at 4.8 GB once waited 45 min to grow into 28672 MB beside 12-15 GB of free memory; print-rs:test once waited 22 min for 18585 MB, a size borrowed from the dashboard's 14.8 GB peak). A run that is the only one running never waits on itself, and neither do runs whose quiet reservations all have to grow at once (they would wait for each other forever and hold the whole queue: the earliest grows first, the others wait for it): if even the bounded size does not fit, the reservation grows to the largest size that fits and says so (`reservation grown from … MB to … MB of the … MB it asked for … the run goes on with 4 of 8 job(s)`); the run keeps only the jobs that memory carries at the learned footprint of one job (never fewer than one), and the plan of the planner process (`--jobs`, local resources, workers, compiler units) shrinks to them. A class that peaks at 14 GB (print-rs:test cold) asks for 18515 MB: that is its own measured bound, 90th percentile of its peaks plus the margin.
   - **Growing a reduced grant**: a Bazel run admitted with fewer jobs than it asked for (`[capacity] admitted run with 1 of 8 job(s): the host cannot carry more right now; the run grows to more jobs once memory frees up`) asks once per heartbeat to grow: it grows only while nothing else of ours waits, after the reservation of its previous growth was observed (its ramp; the admission reservation counts in full while it ramps, so a run may grow right after its admission), on two consecutive heartbeats and to the most jobs whose memory fits with one more job's memory left free; it never shrinks back (`[capacity] run: grown from 1 to 8 of 8 job(s) ...`). Processes that start work afterwards follow the grant row: the Bazel invocation at its capacity point (`--parallel` carries the jobs it may grow into, `--jobs` follows the plan) and every planned compile.
   - **Compile cores** (`🎟️capacity/🧮️units`): a planned compile under a run's grant takes the cores the grant's job memory carries at the unit footprint the rustc broker measured (75th percentile of the newest unit peaks, 2048 MB until 8 units are measured), at least the granted jobs and at most half the host; the broker never holds more than that memory. A 1-job grant with 2048 MB and 900 MB units compiles on 2 cores instead of 1.
   - **Accounting of other runs**: a grant's reservation counts in full while it ramps (its class's time to peak, 60 s for `repo capacity run --memory`), then by what its process tree holds; a holder that holds nothing (`sleep`) therefore stops holding memory after its ramp, while a command that really uses its memory keeps it counted. The `available` of a log line is the host sample, never net of reservations.
   - `SEMIO_HOST_CAPACITY=off` is the escape hatch; wrap ad-hoc heavy commands in `repo capacity run --memory=<MB> -- <command>`.
5. **Tool**:
   - **Bazel** with persistent workers: 2 servers on 16 GB (heap 1536 MB each, a further server only per 4096 MB headroom, idle ones are reaped and exit after 600 s). A repeated selection in a slot skips the planner when a traced witness of everything the planner read still holds (`[bazel] plan reused: N observations … the planner was not started`; `SEMIO_REPO_PLAN_REUSE=off|verify`; a miss says `[bazel] planning again: <reason>`); the change journal (`SEMIO_REPO_CHANGES=on|off`, default on, needs the native watcher, else the always-correct stat walk) makes that check cheap. The hermetic `course:typecheck` is served by a persistent worker service (`RepoHermeticService`, 2 instances, 120 s idle, cold fallback, `--workers=off` forces cold).
   - **Nx**: at most 2 finite daemons; on hosts up to 24 GiB they idle out after 2 min and retire above 1280 MB resident.
   - **Planner skip and host probes**: the traced witness that decides plan reuse was tainted by four read-only host probes of the planner child (`sysctl -n kern.bootsessionuuid` and `-n kern.boottime`, `hdiutil info -plist`, `mount`, and `ps` with any arguments), so no run since the planner skip landed ever printed `plan reused` and every hot no-op planned the same 198 projects again (about 29 of 50 s inside the run child, `tool-setup` 32-48 s). A closed table `hostProbes` of exactly those program and argument vectors (the program a bare name or in `/bin /sbin /usr/bin /usr/sbin`; on Linux the boot identity files `/proc/stat`, `/proc/1/stat`, `/proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id`) is now exempt; `sysctl -w`, `hdiutil attach`, a `ps` from another directory or any other spawn still taints, and the taint line names the command line. Measured since (10-27): a hot pair prints no `cannot be reused later` line and then `[bazel] plan reused: 73500 observations … the planner was not started (1.0 s)`; the planner step of a hot no-op fell from about 29 s to 0.6-1.0 s. Four further causes kept every plan from being sealed and are fixed: a file the witness skips on purpose (the generated workspace, `.cache`) made it "racy", the granted job count and per-run handover variables were part of the key, the plan of a dashboard build evicted the course's, and print's plan read the whole repository. What ends a plan today is the next two bullets. A Windows planner stays unreusable until its `powershell Get-CimInstance Win32_Process` child listing is in the table.
   - **Plan reuse, what keeps a plan** (live): a plan is reused when its key and its witness still hold. The key is the selection, the graph digest, the identity of the running implementation, the scopes and capabilities of the run and the runtime (binary, version, platform). The witness lists what the planner read, one row per fact, and a miss names the first row that moved (`[bazel] planning again: <kind> <path> changed`; kinds: `l` and `s` stat, `d` directory listing, `f` file read, `k` and `K` the kind of an entry, `g` git index listing, `i` git ignore rules, `e` an environment variable, `E` the whole environment, `x` command output, `h` host fact). A plan survives: a content edit of a declared source (the planner records only that a file exists and what kind it is; the publishing parent copies the changed file into the snapshot and rewrites the action fingerprints after every plan, fresh or reused, for 0.1-0.25 s; with `--verbose` it prints `[bazel] source snapshot: N files, 1 captured, N-1 reused`), so a one-line edit of the course takes 30 s and of print 105 s, both `plan reused`; the job count the admission granted (`--parallel=N`); per-run handover variables (graph yield, capacity grant and phase marks, generation pin); ambient host, desktop, agent and session variables (`CLAUDE*`, `ANTHROPIC*`, `__CF*`, `XPC_*`, `TERM_SESSION_ID`, `SECURITYSESSIONID`, `LaunchInstanceID`) and the agent identity (`SEMIO_AGENT_SESSION`, `AI_AGENT`); diagnostic switches such as `SEMIO_REPO_PROFILE`; and the dashboard between two course builds: a slot keeps the package and the plan of its last selection only, a selection returns to the slot it used last (then a never-used slot, then the least recently used), and a dashboard build uses two slots, its own and one for the nested `repo-dashboard-rs:compile`.
   - **Plan reuse, what ends a plan**: a new, removed or renamed file in a glob (`g`, `d`), a new package boundary or an entry that becomes a link or a directory (`k`), changed ignore rules (`i`), a changed input declaration (the graph digest), any other environment variable that changed or appeared (`e NAME changed`, or `E` for the whole environment: exporting a new variable replans once), an edit of the Bazel adapter or the code it loads (the first run after a landing replans once: new implementation identity), a build that writes into a directory the planner listed and git does not ignore, an editor that saves through a temporary file and a rename (the parent directory changed), and a recorded file younger than 3 s when the planner read it. The last one is not recorded at all: `[bazel] this plan cannot be reused later: <file> was modified moments before the planner read it`, and the next run plans again. `SEMIO_REPO_PLAN_REUSE=off` always plans, `verify` always plans and says when the earlier plan would have differed. A plan that has to be made again costs 20-60 s of every run; run the same command again and read the reason of the first one.
   - **Runtime rows without a child** (live since G34): a recorded `runtime` row whose output is a pure function of the executable (`node --version`, `node -p "process.platform.concat(process.arch)"`, `bun --version`; `rustc -vV` and `cargo --version` are not listed because rustup proxies depend on `rust-toolchain.toml`) carries an identity in its witness subject: resolved path, real path, size, times, inode and mode of the program and of the script it runs. The reuse check compares the identity in the process (0.5 ms for all rows) and starts no child; a changed identity, an unlisted row or an older receipt runs the command and compares the digest as before. A hot run starts three processes fewer (about 180 MB at the peak).
   - **Plan walk and ignored directories** (live since G34): the planner's walk of the directories below a plan's roots no longer enters a directory that git ignores as a whole (one `git ls-files --others --ignored --exclude-standard --directory` over the walked roots, 0.05 s, an allowed spawn). That is sound because git never lists an untracked file below an ignored directory, the ignore rules themselves are observed (`i` rows and every `.gitignore` on the way) and a directory appearing or vanishing changes the listing of its parent. The architecture course's own build output `.🧾️store/build.json` therefore no longer ends its plan (before, the first run after a build and the revert of an edit replanned, 45-50 s; now edit and revert of a course source both print `plan reused`, 25 s and 35 s in gate run g34c); a build that writes into a directory git does not ignore still does.
   - **Plan walk, excluded names and the git row** (live, G36-G39): the `g` row (git index listing) digests only the files the planner admits (not `.🧬semio/`, not `.git/`), so a ticket file another agent staged no longer ends a plan (about 30 ms more per check). The source walk keeps a directory with an excluded name (`.git`, `.🧬semio`, `.cache`, `.nx`, `node_modules`, `target`, `dist`, `build`, `coverage`, `.venv`, `__pycache__`) at any depth and asks git once which of them it ignores as a whole (`check-ignore`); the others are walked like any directory, because an untracked file in a nested `.🧬semio` or in an un-ignored `target` was a case of unsound reuse. `.git` itself and the unadmitted root bookkeeping are never entered.
   - **Compilers**: rustc front-end threads follow the admitted cores (`SEMIO_COMPILER_PARALLELISM`).
   - **Test processes** are guarded three ways (`🧪️testing/🟨️bun`): a `bun test` operand without a leading `./` is refused (bun reads it as a filter and scans the whole checkout, holding every directory open: 122,880 files and 2 GB in one measured case); the preload `🪦️orphans` caps the open files of every `bun test` process (8192, `SEMIO_TEST_OPEN_FILES_CEILING`) and ends the process and everything it started when its runner is gone; the owned runners stop a tree at 90 % of the cap by name and stop and name every process a test file left running. `SEMIO_TEST_OPERAND_GUARD=off` and `SEMIO_TEST_ORPHAN_GUARD=off` lift them.
   - **Test runners** start the workers one job of their grant carries, from one policy (`🧪️testing/👷️workers`): memory of the job minus the base of the runner over the measured RSS of a worker (learned per class in `estimates.json`), and the cores of the job times what the runner can share one core with, never fewer than one. Defaults: vitest 4 (cap 8), bun test files 1 (cap 8), Playwright 1 (cap 4), end-to-end stacks 2, Cargo test threads = cores. An override (`SEMIO_VITEST_WORKERS`, `SEMIO_BUN_TEST_WORKERS`, `SEMIO_CARGO_TEST_THREADS`, `SEMIO_PLAYWRIGHT_WORKERS`, `SEMIO_E2E_STACKS`) never leaves the admitted cores; a reduced count prints `[tests] Vitest: 2 of 4 worker(s), bounded by …`. A test file that holds more than a typical file process is declared with its measured peak RSS in `🧪️testing/👷️workers/📏️file-budgets`: the suite runner prices a cohort with its biggest file, starts the files of a cohort only while the megabytes held by the running ones plus the budget of the next fit the share of the grant, and stops a file above 150 % of its budget (3 GB when undeclared) by name; `SEMIO_TEST_FILE_CEILING=off` lifts the ceilings. The same ceilings hold every `bun test` process that the owned command runners start (`🧪️testing/🟨️bun/🧯️ceiling`), whoever the caller is: it is terminated by name above its ceiling, a command that is no `bun test` of the hosting runtime has none.
   - **Action environment** (live): an action process does not inherit your shell; it gets an allowlist. The planner writes it to `runtime.json` of the generated workspace (mode 0600, outside every action key): the names of the classes of the schema `🌿️environment/🏃️action` (host, temporary directories, locale, terminal, CI kind, `SEMIO_*` and `NX_*`, Rust `CARGO*` and `RUST*`, native toolchain `SDKROOT`, `CC*`, `CPATH`, ..., JavaScript `NODE_*`, `BUN_*`, `VITE_*`, `PLAYWRIGHT_*`, Go, Python and .NET, package manager, git, XDG directories, graphics, Windows) plus every name the task graph itself declares. Credential-like names (`*TOKEN*`, `*SECRET*`, `*PASSWORD*`, `*_KEY`, `AUTH`, `COOKIE`, ...) are withheld even inside an allowed class, and so are agent, desktop and session variables, `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`, proxies, `DISPLAY` and `DBUS_*`; on the reference Mac the file holds 46 of 72 shell names. **To give a task a variable, declare it**: an `{"env": "NAME"}` row among the `inputs` of the target or of a named input (as `SEMIO_BUILD_MODE` in `nx.json`), a `valueEnv` entry or an `env` map of the target. The plan collects the declarations (`declared-environment.json` beside the workspace, read again by the run phase; a missing record is refused), delivers them even when credential-like, and the value of a declared variable is part of the action key. A switch exported in an arbitrary shell therefore no longer reaches a Bazel action unless the task declares it (it was an undeclared input of a cached task), and a service or continuous task that read a credential from the shell has to declare it. A name the build tool's own code starts to read must be classified in the fixture of the allowlist, else a law turns red. A hot or cached run executes no action: to prove that a task does not need a withheld variable, run it with `--skip-cache`. The Bazel client keeps its own environment (cache credential helper) and `TEST_*` come from Bazel.
   - **Runtime rows of a key**: a `{"runtime": "<command>"}` input (as `repo tools exec --light bun -- --version` in `sharedGlobals`) keys by the standard OUTPUT of the command only, run without the diagnostic switches (`SEMIO_REPO_PROFILE`, `SEMIO_LEASE_TRACE`, `SEMIO_PROGRESS_DEADLINE_TRACE`, `NODE_DEBUG`, `BUN_CONFIG_VERBOSE_FETCH`) and the ambient variables; standard error never keys, and a command that fails is refused in English and German instead of being keyed. Before, `SEMIO_REPO_PROFILE=json|1` (its profile goes to standard error) re-keyed seven actions of the course build on every run (8 processes, 55-60 s instead of 20 s).

## 🤝️ Working as one of many agents

About 20 agents share one checkout and one machine. What keeps that working:

- **Identity**: `bun repo session open --agent=<name>` (also `session list|status|close`, `close --dead`) gives you a session with its own cargo lane, Bazel slot preference, Nx daemon namespace, test sandbox root, lease owner tag and budget share. Without `--agent` the client's own session variable or the nearest agent ancestor names you. MCP tools take `agent` for the same attribution.
- **Priority class**: agents run as `agent`, fan-out tools as `background` (see the next section); never claim `interactive` from an agent.
- **Queue etiquette**: a waiting run is cheap (door alone, 35-75 MB) and Ctrl+C gives everything back, so wait; do not start the same build twice. Start a graph-loading run only when `bun repo leases status | grep -c WAITING` is below 4, one at a time. Use `SEMIO_BUILD_GRAPH_POLICY=current` for read-only questions. Never start `bun`, `tsc`, Python or `find` scans that read the tree or the ticket `🗑️generated` folders outside `repo capacity run`; ad-hoc scripts stream their input and stay below 500 MB RSS; kill your own pids before you finish, never someone else's.
- **Staging edits to the build tool**: the build library, the process module and the launcher are edited by many lanes at once. While a landing gate is open (wave 1026g), edits there are staged, not live: an anchored patch (every anchor must exist exactly once, else it refuses and writes nothing; new files first), a revert, base hashes (apply refuses when a file changed since staging), a graph-free verify script and laws with an independent oracle, run on a mirror of the tree and never through a symlink farm or the live guide (two write-through incidents on 10-26, one of them into this file): `python3 -I .🧬semio/🦑️repo/🎫️tickets/🎆️26/🌙️10/☀️18/GET-CODEBASE-RUNNING-END-TO-END-AGAIN/mirror-tools/mirror.py create <lane>-<what> [--batch <stage dir or command>]` makes a copy-on-write copy with no symlink into the live tree (`run`, `diff`, `list`, `drop` follow; names of at most 8 characters; drop only your own), on macOS writes below the live repository are denied and on every platform a guard exits 97 when a live file or directory listing changed; the lane hands it over with `FOR gate-g: batch ready <path>`; the gate applies one batch at a time, runs one official product build (course, dashboard, print in turn) and reverts on red. Exceptions are type-error fixes and a fix of a red you caused (announce it as FIX-OF-RED with the failing run). Outside a gate: compile-atomic (module complete and tested, then the call-site switch in one edit), re-read before you edit.
- **Sealed snapshot**: `SEMIO_REPO_SEAL=sealed` runs the tool from a content-addressed snapshot of its own sources, so a half-finished peer edit can neither stop your command nor move the implementation digest that keys the graph memo and the Bazel plan; `live` captures the tree as before; `promote` promotes now and runs nothing (exit 1 when blocked); the default is `defaultMode` in `🏗️build/🏛️sealed/🔣️.json`. Promotion is explicit: a command runs the promoted snapshot and says at most every 5 minutes that the live tree has unpromoted changes; the landing gate runs `SEMIO_REPO_SEAL=promote bun repo` after every landing that touches the tool, an agent that edits the tool uses `SEMIO_REPO_SEAL=live` for its own runs, and a snapshot older than a day promotes a drifted tree by itself. `SEMIO_REPO_IMPLEMENTATION` is the dashboard's repo-domain switch (`rust`|`go`), not this one. Details: `🏗️build/🏛️sealed/README.md`.
- **Fleet status**: `bun repo fleet status` (`--json`, `--limit=<n>`; MCP tool `fleet_status`) is one read-only picture in under a second without a lease or a graph: who runs what (phase, counter, time left, age, priority, code freshness, memory admitted against really used), every queue with a waiting-time estimate, Bazel servers, storage against its budgets, sealed implementation, graph generation and findings (stuck holders, pressure, long waits, low disk). Start here, then `bun repo leases status` or `bun repo capacity status` for detail.
- **Memory budget** (16 GB host, measured 10-26):

  | Part | Bound |
  |---|---|
  | waiting run | door alone, 35-75 MB; loader 255 MB |
  | free-memory floor | 2048 MB always left free |
  | graph builder | 3.7-5.0 GB flat in vivo (15-29 GB before the abort-listener leak fix); target 2 GB not met; a refresh of 355 owners peaks at 10.3 GB of process tree since the graph child's heap is fixed at 2 GiB (14-16 GB before) |
  | Bazel | 2 servers, JVM peak 0.75 GB fleet-wide, persistent worker 98 MB |
  | Nx | 2 finite daemons, drain above 1280 MB |
  | test workers | one policy per runner, never beyond the grant |
  | test files | declared peak per file; a process stopped at 150 % (3 GB undeclared), by name |

- **Disk budget**: see the disk section; set the budget per machine, run `cache gc --dry-run --area=…` before anything else, delete the `🗑️generated` folder of your ticket when you are done with it (outside the ledger); the purge law in the disk section says what `repo ticket purge-artifacts` and `repo clean` may remove, and they never touch anything else of a ticket.

**When it goes wrong**

| Symptom | Meaning and action |
|---|---|
| exit 75 | declared restart (outdated implementation, format stamp moved, graph yield, file churn, a source renamed mid-run). The front door restarts by itself up to 6 times with back-off 0.25, 1, 4, 12, 30, 30 s (`Restarting with the current implementation (2 of 6)`); a wrapper that surfaces it: run again, do not loop faster. |
| `Foreign native image mounted inside the parent … Detach the device <d>` | a test mounted an image inside a managed `.cache/b` parent; its sandbox must live outside (OS temp directory). Detach the named device through its owner, never force-unmount; finished scratch images idle 15 min are swept at the next `repo` command. |
| `graph: <project> is withheld: <file> does not parse (sha256 …)` or `… depends on projects that this graph snapshot does not contain` | the project is held back, not broken: fix or restore the named file and run again (the next generation heals it), or run again for projects created or removed during the build. A task of a withheld project says why instead of `Unknown build task`. |
| `The runtime registry declares contract N, this launcher implements contract M. Rebuild and reinstall the launcher with: …` | the installed Go launcher is older than the registry; run the printed installer once (`🚀️launcher/📥️install`); there is one install location. |
| `Refusing to wait: <lease> (<mode>) has been unavailable for N s, held by process P for M s while this process holds <other> exclusively …` (`LeaseOrderErrorV1`; also `Self-deadlock: …` and `Deadlock detected: …`) | the lock-order law refuses a wait that would block everyone behind you: hold-and-wait after a 15 s grace, a request for a lease you already hold, or a cycle (this request is chosen as the victim so the others proceed). It is not a hang: release the held resource before waiting, or take the awaited one first. A dashboard rebuild no longer meets it behind a running dashboard. `SEMIO_LEASE_ORDER` set to `off`, `observe` or `enforce` overrides every rule at once (default: hold-and-wait, self-deadlock and cycle `enforce`, out-of-order `observe`); `SEMIO_LEASE_ORDER_POLICY` names another policy file. |
| `[repo cargo] the job-token pool named by <variable> cannot be reached by this process (<reason>); the command runs on its own single job slot …` | the build script or compiler wrapper inherited a job pool it cannot open (stale descriptor numbers, a vanished fifo, a disabled pool); it runs serially on its own implicit slot, which can only be slower, never wrong. Start the build from `repo cargo` (its launcher pool is a fifo and always reachable) and not from a shell that kept a stale `MAKEFLAGS` or `CARGO_MAKEFLAGS`. `bin/jobserver-empty` is a FIFO inside the cache root: a recursive `cp -R` or rsync of the cache copies it as a FIFO. |
| `[tests] <file> holds N open files in process P (<command>) and was stopped at its ceiling of C open files`, `[tests] the runner of this test process (P) is gone: ending process P and everything it started`, `[tests] <file> left N process(es) running after it ended; they were stopped: …` | the three guards of `bun test` processes (`🧪️testing/🟨️bun`): every `bun test` process caps its own open files (default 8192, `SEMIO_TEST_OPEN_FILES_CEILING`; the largest honest test needs 320), the owned runner stops a tree at 90 % of the cap by name, a test process whose runner died ends itself and everything it started (checked every 250 ms by a worker thread, +7 MB), and processes a finished test file left behind are stopped and named. Fix the test (close the files, stop the services it starts); `SEMIO_TEST_ORPHAN_GUARD=off` lifts the child side. The runner side tracks process trees through `ps`: on Windows only the child side runs. |
| `[tests] <file> held N MB and was stopped at its ceiling of M MB (K MB declared in the test file budgets)` or `… (not declared in the test file budgets)` | a test file went above 150 % of its declared peak (3001 MB when undeclared, the runaway guard) and its process tree was ended once, by name; it holds for suite runs and for every `bun test` process the owned command runners start, whoever calls (`🧪️testing/🟨️bun/🧯️ceiling`; a process is held to the biggest budget of its files). German: `… belegte N MB und wurde an seiner Obergrenze von M MB angehalten`. If the peak is honest, declare it with the measured RSS in `🧪️testing/👷️workers/📏️file-budgets` (at least 64 MB); otherwise lower the memory of the test. `SEMIO_TEST_FILE_CEILING=off` lifts all ceilings, `SEMIO_TEST_FILE_BUDGETS=<json>` replaces the table. |
| `FAIL: tests touched the real repository root <path>: …` (Go tests) | a Go test resolved or bound the real checkout (the abort names the test). Make the test use a root it owns (`sandbox.BindRoot`, a fixture repository), never make it pass with `SEMIO_SANDBOX_SCRATCH=keep`; fixture tickets or sessions in `git status` of `.🧬semio` are the sign that an older test wrote through. |
| `[lease-order] Lock order violated: <requested> (<class>, rank N) is requested while <held> (<class>, rank M) is held …` | a diagnostic (default `observe`), never fatal: a process asked for a lease of a lower rank than one it holds, and the build goes on. It is a defect of the declared or the acquisition order, not of your command. Report the pair of classes from `violations.jsonl` of the lease registry; `SEMIO_LEASE_ORDER=enforce` turns it into an error for tests. A hot course run and a cold dashboard build print none; the two pairs of the Rust test path of print (`cargo:configuration-view` under the Cargo store lease, `cargo:configuration-publication` under the compiler writer) are fixed by taking the leases in their declared order. |
| `[bazel] this plan cannot be reused later: the planner read something that cannot be checked: spawned <program> <arguments>` | the planner skip is off for this run: the planner child started a program that is not one of the known read-only host probes, so the next run plans again (slower, correct, no `plan reused` line). The line names the full command line: a read-only host probe of the build tool itself belongs in the closed table `hostProbes` of the witness policy (`🏗️build/🔭️witness`), any other spawn is an input the planner has to declare. |
| `[bazel] planning again: <reason>` | the planner runs again (20-60 s) because the earlier plan of this slot cannot be reused. A first run of a selection prints no line. `selection, graph, implementation or environment changed`: the key moved (another graph generation, an edit of the build tool, another tool or mode). `<kind> <path> changed`: a recorded fact moved (kinds in the plan reuse bullets; `e NAME changed` names an ordinary environment variable of your shell, `d <dir> changed` a directory the planner listed, `g` a glob that gained or lost a file). `the generated workspace changed`, `the earlier plan record is unreadable`: the record of the slot was damaged. `[bazel] session slot sN: the package of the slot is replaced in place for a different selection` in front of it means another selection used the slot since. Run the same command again: when only one input moved, the second run prints `plan reused`. |
| `[bazel] this plan cannot be reused later: <file> was modified moments before the planner read it` or `… a fact changed while the planner ran: <detail>` | the plan itself is correct but is not recorded: the next run plans again and only the one after prints `plan reused`. Cause: a recorded file younger than 3 s (a peer or your editor was writing it) or a fact that moved while the planner ran. Run again when the tree is quiet. Files the witness skips by design (the generated workspace, `.cache`) never cause it. |
| `The change journal cannot vouch: <reason>` or `The file watcher cannot vouch for every change (<detail>); the tree is walked.` | harmless: the journal refuses and the caller does the complete stat walk (slower, never wrong). Causes: portable watcher backend, failed canary or watch limit (`untrusted`), a root in a region the watcher never reports (`excluded`), a service restarted since the last run (`epoch`), a gap, lost events or more than N changed files (`gap`, `lost`, `overflow`). Use the native watcher, raise the OS watch limit, or switch the journal off with `SEMIO_REPO_CHANGES=off`. |
| `graph: the memo cannot serve this request (<reason>); the graph is planned` | harmless: the graph memo refused and the graph child ran as without it (slower, never wrong). `light`: the record of this request is not sealed yet (this run seals it, the next one is served). `changed: <path>`: a file or directory the slice read changed since the record, the first one is named; an executing build may rewrite files the slice reads (the first run after a real build can refuse once), and so do peer edits below directories the slice reads. `pointer`: a newer graph generation was published. `journal: <detail>`: the change journal cannot vouch (restarted service, gap, lost events, overflow, no service: the causes of the row above) and the next run takes a new cursor. `stale`: a fact outside the journal's sight (a link target, `node_modules`) no longer holds; planning the graph refreshes it. `generation`, `plan`, `pin`, `error`: a record file is missing, damaged or cannot be re-owned. A request never planned before prints nothing. `changed` or `light` again and again without anybody editing: a new implementation of the build tool (a landing) or a restarted journal service starts a new record. `SEMIO_BUILD_GRAPH_MEMO=off` switches the memo off. |
| `graph: the memo of generation N still describes generation M …`, `graph: the memo re-checked the N facts that read the M changed paths …`, `graph: N derived facts that read them were asked again by a child process …` | information, not a warning: the memo served the graph although the generation moved (carry), a file changed (edit) or a derived TypeScript-program fact had to be asked again (a child `build:graph-derive`, 5-7 s and about 2 GB for the course). They come before the usual `served from the memo` line. When the check does not hold, the next line is the refusal `changed: <path>` and the graph is planned. |

## 🧰️ First run on a new machine

```bash
bun repo preflight                  # what this host lacks, with the exact remedy of each (EN or DE)
bun repo tools setup all            # pinned Nx and Bazel, every toolchain, the Node native core, then the compiler helpers
```

- **Prerequisites are named at once**: `rust` is required on every platform, `developer-tools` on macOS (`xcode-select -p`; remedy `xcode-select --install`), `msvc-linker` on Windows (`link.exe` on PATH, `VSINSTALLDIR` or `vswhere` with the x64 or ARM64 component; remedy: the Visual Studio Build Tools workload), `cc` and `pkg-config` on Linux (`build-essential` in the devcontainer image). Each remedy names the exact command; the bootstrap scripts (sh, ps1) install rustup and the pinned toolchain.
- **Node native core** (`node-native-core`): Node's system calls come from a core built once per source digest from the pinned Rust toolchain; `repo tools setup all` builds it (a built core is left alone, a prebuilt one named by `SEMIO_RUNTIME_NATIVE_ADDON` stands in). Every missing prerequisite is reported at once, in English and German, before any compiler starts: `The Node native core cannot be built on <platform> <arch>: missing <list>; run repo preflight, it names the remedy of each`.
- **Compiler helpers**: after a successful `repo tools setup` the optional step `compiler-helpers` (1200 s, all platforms) builds the rustc scheduler and the unit-cache compiler wrapper (a `/bin/sh` stand-in on POSIX, a native executable on Windows) inside the same compiler store, capacity grant and storage custody that `repo cargo` uses (`Preparing compiler-helpers: …`); a failure is reported and the setup goes on (`… could not be prepared (<reason>); it is built on the first compile instead`). Before, they were built on the first `repo cargo`.
- Not verified: a live `repo tools setup all` with the step (it runs Cargo under the capacity gate) and the Windows and Linux branches of the helper build; the probes run for every platform through injected ports (23 hand-written scenarios and a Python oracle).

## 🚦️ Priority, leases and stuck holders

- **Classes** `interactive > agent > background`: a person at a terminal outside an agent session is `interactive`, everything else `agent`, the MCP fan-out tools (`test`, `run_many`, `affected` and the `run` of `incremental`, `conformance`, `units`, `bench`, `concurrency`) export `background`; `SEMIO_LEASE_PRIORITY=interactive|agent|background` overrides. A ticket is filed 60 min earlier (interactive) or later (background) than it arrived and served in filed order, so overtaking is bounded and nothing starves. The capacity queue reads the same class (and treats a class whose median run is under 120 s as interactive).
- **`bun repo leases status`** (`--json`, `--all`): holders, waiters, who blocks whom, cycles, the capacity queue. A second line per exclusive holder shows priority, code age, phase and counters (`inference 120 of 566`), time left (from its own counter, else the median of earlier holds of the same kind) and the state `progressing`, `silent` (1 min), `STUCK` (no published progress and no CPU use of the holder or its children, probed every 30 s, for 20 min on the graph builder, 60 min elsewhere), `STALE` (loaded older code of a format stamp than the newest process) or `unknown` (no live registry entry carries its standing; never stopped). Waiters see the same in their `lease-queue` line, English or German. A busy holder is never stuck.
- **`SEMIO_LEASE_RECLAIM=report|request|terminate`** (default `request`): `report` only shows; `request` asks a stuck holder to give the resource up (it ends with exit 75, the outermost command restarts); `terminate` additionally stops one that ignored the request for 2 min and made no progress (matching OS start time, signalable, never itself; SIGTERM, SIGKILL of the tree after 15 s; `reclaim.jsonl`; not on Windows). A holder on newer code asks a stale holder to yield; a healthy holder is never asked.
- **Lock order** (live, `🔒️leases/🧭️order/🔣️policy.json`): every lease belongs to a class with a rank, and a task that holds one may only wait for a lease of a higher rank, unless the policy lists the edge. Ranks: `pin` 0 (the pinned graph snapshot `build-graph:` and the Rust compiler code `rust-code:`, held shared around a whole build; nobody ever waits for them, so they constrain nothing and may precede any lease), `toolchain` 1 (Rust toolchain home and configuration view), `tool-singleton` 2 (Bazel binary download, session slots `bazel-slot-sN`, restore and metrics, tool activation), `cargo-coordination` 2 (Cargo registry, preparation, source admission, build leases), `cargo-custody` 2, `descriptor-custody` 2 (the Rust unit descriptor namespace and its maintenance gate), `compiler-capacity` 3, `graph` 4, `namespace` 5, `artifact` 6 and `native-whole-retirement` 6, `image` 7 (fixed images, native storage, the compiler writer custody `repo:compiler-writer:`), `registry-fetch` 8 and `bazel-picker` 9 (`bazel-slot-gate`, the innermost lock: a short critical section that only reads and writes slot files, so no blocking lease may be requested while it is held). `SEMIO_LEASE_ORDER=off|observe|enforce` overrides every rule at once. By default an out-of-order request is only observed: it prints `[lease-order] Lock order violated: <requested> (<class>, rank N) is requested while <held> (<class>, rank M) is held <mode>. Acquire locks in ascending rank or release <held> first.` once per class pair and process and records every violation in `lease-registry/<id>/violations.jsonl` of the host-capacity cache directory. Hold-and-wait (a holder that waits more than 15 s for another lease), self-deadlock, cycles and the strict waits of the compiler writer are enforced and refuse. A warning means the declared order and the acquisition order disagree: fix the code when a lease really nests the other way, fix the policy when the held lease is one nobody waits for (that is how `pin` and `bazel-picker` came to be: a hot course run printed one such line per run and every one was a wrong declaration, not a deadlock risk).

## 🔥️ Warm caches and what invalidates them

A task key is its declared inputs, the inferred command closure, tool pins and the mode (`prototype`/`release` are different keys). Edits fall into two kinds:

| Edit | Effect |
|---|---|
| **Scheduling code**: leases, capacity, store custody, process supervision, retry observation, progress and texts, process launch (the seven domains of `🏗️build/📥️source/🔏️closure/🗓️scheduling/🔣️.json`) | no action key moves; Nx re-keys at most the 1171 of 12817 tasks that read that code on purpose (tests, lints); Rust targets stay keyed on `native-build` (it assembles Cargo arguments) |
| **Output-producing code**: command scripts, generators, native-build, inference, schemas, product sources | re-keys everything whose closure contains it; about 3.7k of 4020 projects contain build-library files in their closure |
| Policy numbers: `hostCapacity`, `workers`, `remoteCache`, `remoteExecution` of `🏗️build/🔣️policy.json`, `storage` of `⚡️caching/🔣️policy.json` | nothing (sectional digest) |
| Ambient host, agent, desktop or session variable, a diagnostic switch (`SEMIO_REPO_PROFILE`, `SEMIO_LEASE_TRACE`), the job count granted to the run | nothing: no action key moves and the Bazel plan is reused (the value of a variable a task declares as `env` input is part of its key) |
| Comment, rewritten specifier or import of a file the shared walks already reach | graph: derived facts re-ask the question, the answer is equal, 0 projects re-inferred; a new file in a closure is a real change |
| Peer edit of an unrelated file, or a file renamed mid-run | observed again (bounded retries: 6 reads, 5 snapshots, 4 builds), else exit 75 and an automatic restart; never a failed run |
| Byte edit of a TypeScript product source that keeps its imports | graph: derived facts hold, 0 projects re-inferred (the declared closure of a TypeScript project no longer reads the bytes of its program files); Bazel: plan reused, the affected action re-executes (course one-line edit 30 s, the revert 45-50 s) |
| Byte edit of a Rust product source | Bazel: plan reused, the test action re-executes (print one-line edit 105 s, the revert 20 s) |

- A library edit lands as one graph generation. Measured at the gate: 7 to 170 projects re-inferred (170 in 355 s), a one-project change 34 s. A framework-wide edit re-infers about 566 projects, which took 60-120 min with a builder at 5-13 GB before the memory and early-cutoff fixes; not re-measured since.
- The source closure of the build tool (about 1450 files) is captured once per user and remembered (`~/Library/Caches/semio-repo`, `$XDG_CACHE_HOME`, `%LOCALAPPDATA%`; hit 0.1 s against 8-30 s under load; any edit of the closure is a miss; `SEMIO_REPO_SOURCE_MEMO=on|off`).
- **Change journal service** (`SEMIO_REPO_CHANGES=on|off`, default on; native watcher `semio-repo-watch`, `SEMIO_REPO_WATCH_BINARY` names another): one detached service per checkout and user writes the append-only log of file changes that the plan-reuse check and the dependency verification ask; it ends after 2 h idle. Its memory cap is 128 MiB: with the former 50 MiB a burst of a few thousand rows (20 agents editing) ended it with reason `memory` within seconds on the live tree, so no consumer ever got a journal (a fresh service is 33-37 MB, one million rows peak at 62-68 MB). It stops itself, with its journal, when its workspace (`root-gone`) or its journal directory (`directory-gone`) vanished, and a watcher stop that never returns cannot keep it alive (stop wait 3 s, exit 9 s after SIGTERM). Leaked `bun` + `semio-repo-watch` pairs with parent 1 under `/var/folders/…/T/reuse-*` come from older code: they idle out after 2 h, or stop the `bun` pid with SIGTERM (never `pkill`).
- **Where the journal lives** (live since G34): per user and checkout in the user's cache directory (macOS `~/Library/Caches/semio/repo/<id>/journal`, Linux `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/semio-repo-<uid>/<id>/journal` or `~/.cache/semio/repo/<id>/journal`, Windows `%LOCALAPPDATA%\semio\repo\<id>\journal`), no longer below `TMPDIR`. Bazel gives its actions a `TMPDIR` of their own, so a nested `repo` run used to start a second journal service with its own watcher, graph memo and vouch cursors; now parent and nested run share one. The first run after the move misses once; services under the former temporary directory idle out after 2 h (stop their `bun` pid with SIGTERM, never `pkill`).
- TypeScript: warm incremental `tsc` is 6-7 CPU-s per check (state file per owner and compiler). The hermetic Bazel check is 24-41 CPU-s cold and 0.4-5 CPU-s in the persistent worker (graph-free corpus of 22 edit steps, identical diagnostics; official before/after not run). Bazel actions are shared across slots through one disk cache; a different selection in a slot costs about 10 s instead of a 28 s JVM restart.
- **Proving a narrowed key (read oracle)**: a task's declared inputs are only trustworthy when a run shows that it reads nothing else (`🏗️build/📥️inputs/🔭️read-oracle`; `read-oracle --root DIR --allow-list FILE … [--backend auto|seatbelt|landlock|mirror] [--max-profiles N] -- COMMAND…`). A key of 12,000 to 26,000 declared files could not be proven because Seatbelt refuses profiles above 65,535 bytes; now directories collapse into one rule, a profile holds at most 600 rules (less when the kernel refuses), a larger plan is split into runs that each grant everything declared and refuse a share of the undeclared files (the union is as strict as the whole plan), and a plan beyond `--max-profiles` (default 4) hands over to the mirror and says so on the progress channel (output equivalence, no read trace; `--max-profiles 8` proves `print:test-viz` with the kernel). Two defects found under load are fixed: the unified log was read after a fixed 2.5 s pause, so at load 40 a denial was missed and the run reported `clean`; it is now read only after a marker denial shows that it has flushed (60 s limit, note `log:unflushed`). The `log show` window used a local time stamp that began an hour early under `bun test` (TZ=UTC) and would begin in the future west of UTC; it is `--last <n>s` now. A command started with an already aborted signal starts nothing. Planning costs 6-16 s CPU for the three huge keys (dsl-derive-rs:generate-schema-index 12,246 files, framework:test-source-text 18,965, print:test-viz 26,058); no real oracle run of them yet.
- **Dependency verification asks the journal**: every run verifies the dependency generation receipt twice, a complete pass and a recheck, each over about 79,000 frontier rows, 5,600 input sources and 327 manifest texts (3.7-6.7 s and 5.4-7.7 s wall at load 33). With the change journal the first look of a receipt only takes a cursor and the second pass is answered by the journal; from the next look on both passes skip every path no change touched. `SEMIO_REPO_PROFILE=1` marks `dependencies.vouch` as `journal` (answered), `cursor` (first look, other receipt or restarted service), `walk:<reason>` (the journal refused: complete walks as before) or `off` (`SEMIO_REPO_CHANGES=off`). A vouch only narrows: paths in `.git`, `.cache`, `node_modules`, `target`, outside the workspace or below symbolic links are always walked, the cursor is stored only after both passes held and only in the journal's private runtime directory (never in the checkout), and any doubt gives today's complete walk. Measured on the live receipt: CPU of the two passes 2.2 s to 0.65 s, wall at load 42 15.3 s to 6.9-8.9 s (simulated end to end from the measured tiers). Not measured on a real run: the second `SEMIO_REPO_PROFILE=1 bun repo dependencies ensure` must show `dependencies.vouch` = `journal` and `dependencies.verify` plus `dependencies.recheck` well under 1 s.

## ✏️ Editing the build tool while others build

- **Live**: a run finishes with the implementation it loaded; an edit it did not load restarts the outermost command with exit 75 (at most 6 times; `SEMIO_STALE_REEXEC` counts). See the many-agents section for how to land an edit.
- Scheduling-domain edits (table above) cost nobody a rebuild; anything else re-keys and, after a framework-wide edit, re-infers the graph.
- **Numbers and texts live in schemas**: the evening convergence moved hard-coded values into policy JSON next to their schema (read-oracle `memoryLimitMegabytes` and `tailBytes`; authorizer memo 4 MiB and 24 lanes; TypeScript check retention 2 programs, at most 8, 2 GiB, 120 s; the frontier walk; store-bound `probePatienceMs`; host-capacity `wait.jitterBasisPoints` 2500; unit-cache lock and pool back-off `pollBackoffBasisPoints` 15000, the same integers as the old factor 1.5; change-journal `startPollMilliseconds` 20; sealed `maximumTextCharacters`, `maximumPathCharacters`, `maximumCommandLineCharacters`) and made the read-oracle CLI and refusals, the machine-budget policy errors (`machine.policy.*`) and the change-journal service refusal (`The change journal cannot vouch: <reason> / Der Änderungsjournal-Dienst kann nicht einstehen: <reason>`) bilingual. Audit rules: every exported symbol has a docstring that starts with an emoji unique within its file, no comments inside definitions, no English-only user text. Internal invariant throws and the progress keys `nx-tooling:census|probe|wait|retire` stay English by decision.
- **Sealed** (`SEMIO_REPO_SEAL=sealed` is opt-in; the default stays `live` until the flip, the last batch to land; `SEMIO_REPO_IMPLEMENTATION` is the dashboard's `rust|go` selector and no longer a sealing switch): a promotion needs the tree to parse, load and pass two self-checks; a broken edit keeps the previous snapshot working and names `path:line:col` (EN and DE). Limits: runtime faults that parse and load are not caught; a light command costs about 1 s; Windows and Linux untested.

## 💾️ Disk budget

```bash
bun repo cache status [--budget=40GiB] [--json]    # ledger + machine plan: quotas per store, bytes to evict, bytes blocked by holders
bun repo cache gc --dry-run [--area=retired,undeclared]
bun repo cache dedupe --dry-run [--area=bazel-outputs]
bun repo cache verify --deep
```

- Budget precedence: `--budget` > `SEMIO_CACHE_BUDGET_BYTES` > file `🎚️machine-budget.json` in the repository cache root > default 121.8 GiB (below the sum of the store floors it is refused); a host free-space floor of 15 GiB shrinks the effective budget. The default is derived, not chosen: the declared image capacities (97.6 GiB: rust 48, products 24, tools 8, dependencies 8, graph 6, diagnostics 2, nx 1.5 GiB, leases 64 MiB) plus the two loose roots (repository 24 GiB, native 256 MiB), the most a workspace with 6 build and 4 service slots and 16 Cargo lanes may hold; a law pins the sum. Quotas are weighted max-min fair per store, each weight being the share of the store in that total (rust 39.4 %, products 19.7 %, tools 6.6 %, dependencies 6.6 %, graph 4.9 %, diagnostics 1.6 %, nx 1.2 %, leases 0.05 %, loose 19.9 %): at the default every store may fill exactly its declared capacity, a lower budget shrinks all of them in proportion. A machine that cannot spare that much (a 16 GB laptop with a small disk) sets the budget per machine, for example `SEMIO_CACHE_BUDGET_BYTES=64GiB`.
- `cache gc` is lock-aware: it re-measures a unit before removing it and never removes one held by an SQLite lock, a queue ticket, a lease, used within the area's quiet time or changed since the scan. Always `--dry-run` first. `--area` is the safe form while peers edit the library; a full `gc` can fail with a restart under churn (fix landed, not re-run live).
- `cache gc --area=nx-tooling,nx-tmp` bounds the Nx toolkit store: the 8 newest generations of `.cache/b/t/m/nx` stay (3 while the tools image is over its machine quota or has under 2.5 GiB free), plus the current activation, every generation a live reader holds (its `nx-tooling:<identity>` lease, probed by a try-lock) and every one younger than 10 minutes; the rest is retired oldest first, at most 16 per run. Retiring needs the whole tools image to itself, so the run polls for an idle image (no queue: nobody is held back) for 90 s (`SEMIO_NX_TOOLING_IDLE_WAIT_MS`, 0 to 600000), otherwise says so and leaves the work to the next run; the Nx storage preparation starts one detached collector per 15 minutes when less than 2.5 GiB are free. Runs hold the tools image for their whole lifetime, so it was never idle while the fleet ran: collect in a quiet window. `nx-tmp` removes `nx/tmp/tmp-<pid>-*` install trees whose process is gone and that stayed unused for an hour (enforced: -825 MiB). `--dry-run` only plans.
- `cache dedupe` replaces identical files by copy-on-write clones (never copies, never hardlinks; a volume that cannot clone keeps everything) in `bazel-outputs`, `bazel-workspaces`, `vite-products`, `tools`; files used within 1 h, volatile names and files changed since hashing are skipped. Measured: 2.75 GiB real gain on the products image.
- Enforcement: a declared area is enforced, not only declared. Every heavy command reads the size ledger (no tree is walked) and starts, detached at low priority and single-flight, `repo cache gc --area=…` when it is due: every collectable area, the retired roots and the undeclared entries older than 14 days every 12 hours, only the areas recorded over their budget every 30 minutes (stamp, log and status file `budget-sweep.*` in the `build` area, intervals in the ledger schema). Areas an owner manages keep their collector: the Cargo unit cache trims its parts to shares of the image (objects 25 %, lanes 20 %, admission memos 3 %: at most 23.0 of the 24 GiB it is granted; memos live at most 7 days) at every collection, collects hard above 87.5 % used down to 75 % and, between 75 % and 87.5 %, maintains once when its watcher starts and every 10 minutes; the Nx toolkit store needs a quiet tools image (see `nx-tooling` above). Cache images open only for the checkout root: a command started inside a module directory is refused in English and German instead of creating a second image set there. A law scans the tracked sources for writers below `.cache` as it does for the repository cache root. The tools image holds 8 GiB of content while its logical limit is 64 GiB, because hardlinked toolchains count once per name; the control directory is bounded at 8 MiB because it holds the 4 MiB metadata headroom file. Sizes of nested areas are apparent bytes (a cloned file counts at each place, `cache dedupe` clones on purpose): the physical use of an image is the partition row of `cache status`, so the Bazel workspaces and outputs report more than the products image holds.
- **Graph memo and journal state** (live, G40): the graph memo directory keeps the newest 8 records and then drops the oldest record while the records and the files they name exceed 1 GiB (the newest always stays; files no kept record names go after 60 s); a removal driven by bytes prints one line (`[repo graph] the graph memo exceeded its 1024 MiB budget: removed … older slice(s) …`, German `Das Graph-Memo überschritt sein Budget von 1024 MiB: …`). When a journal service starts it removes the state directories next to its own in the cache container (`<cache>/semio/repo/<id>` or `semio-repo-<uid>/<id>`, 12-hex ids) whose lock no service holds, after 7 days without a change (10 minutes when the worktree root they recorded is gone) and logs `[repo journal] removed N abandoned change journal state director(ies) (X MiB) …`; a custom layout is never swept.
- New macOS images are created sparse (64 MiB, then resized: cost = content; live). The existing fixed images (about 98 GiB on this machine) stay fully allocated until recreated in a quiet window (helper ready, not applied). `~/.sinc` and ticket `🗑️generated` folders are outside the ledger.
- **Purging ticket output (the one purge law)**: only the direct `🗑️generated` folder of a CLOSED ticket (manifest `status` exactly `closed`) is ever eligible, whatever the size of anything else in the ticket (inputs, scripts, configs and reports stay). `repo ticket purge-artifacts [<ticket>]` plans and `--apply` removes (no argument = every closed ticket, `--all` is gone): files above 5 MiB and folders above 10 MiB; closing a ticket purges its own output with the same guards. `repo clean` applies by default, so run `repo clean --dry` first; it removes everything below `🗑️generated` of closed tickets (plus the classes it always had), and it can no longer remove files of an open ticket. Never removed: a file the git index tracks (a folder holding one is kept, its other contents judged alone; a failing `git ls-files` fails closed as `tracked-unknown`); a ticket that has any file younger than 600 s (`live-writer`, a future mtime counts as young; apply re-walks and refuses when the folder changed after the plan); a ticket that is not closed (`not-closed`); links (never followed; a root that is a link or a file is skipped as `symlink-root` or `not-directory`). The old rules (whole-ticket size sweeps and an open-ticket bypass in `repo clean`) fit the 17:16 loss of 8,024 tracked files and the 17:45 wipe of live output; one 18-case fixture, an independent Python decision table and real `git` and glob oracles now hold Go, Rust and TypeScript to the same plan. Open: recovery of the lost monolith-split files is the user's call; `repo clean` still applies by default.
- **Free space is asked one way**: `freeSpaceSyncV1(path, {nearest?})` answers the free bytes (and the volume size where known) or `undefined` for unknown (the reader fails, an incoherent row, a missing path; `nearest` asks the nearest existing ancestor; on Windows `GetDiskFreeSpaceExW`), never a made-up number, and each site decides what unknown means: the lease sweep counts it as pressure, `cache dedupe` reports an unknown gain, the host free-bytes reader and the doctor storage probe answer `null`, elastic image sizing falls back to the minimum capacity, Bazel session pressure asks the nearest ancestor, and the unit-cache collection used to treat an unreadable volume as empty and capacity-sized without saying so and now keeps the same numbers but marks `unmeasured:true` and collects nothing on a guess. 6 of the 18 files with a production `statfsSync` call go through it; the other 12 stay raw, each named with its reason in a fixture, and a census law compares the raw calls with that list in TypeScript and in an independent Python walk.

## 🦀️ Rust lanes

`bun repo cargo --store=shared --configuration=prototype|release -- <cargo args>`: every agent gets a private lane over one content-addressed unit cache (`SEMIO_CARGO_LANE`, `SEMIO_UNIT_CACHE_SLOTS`), so different crates build concurrently (measured 2.0x for two crates) and a one-line edit of the dashboard binary rebuilds in 44 s (23 CPU-s, 1.3 GB). The cargo part of a run is bounded by the admission: `1 of 10 cores admitted` also bounds rustc threads, and Cargo's own job pool is handed on: build scripts and the compiler wrapper never run more compile jobs than the grant. A pool that Cargo or another parent made is an anonymous PIPE named by descriptors, not by the environment (a launcher fifo or a Windows semaphore is pure environment and always worked); the `/bin/sh` stand-in of a build script now passes an OPEN pair on under the same numbers, and an unreachable pool (stale numbers, missing fifo, a disabled pool) is replaced by an empty first-party fifo (`bin/jobserver-empty` in the unit-cache root) with `-j1` and `NUM_JOBS=1`, so the command runs serially on its own slot instead of inventing a private pool (`cc` made `NUM_JOBS`, rustc 32 slots) and prints `[repo cargo] the job-token pool named by <variable> cannot be reached by this process (<reason>); the command runs on its own single job slot instead of starting more jobs than were admitted`. Measured with a counting compiler (4 crates x 8 C files, most compile jobs at once, plain Cargo / before / after): Cargo's own pipe, grant 4: 4 / 16 / 4; outer pipe, grant 3: 3 / 6 / 3; the same pipe on other descriptor numbers: 3 / 9 / 3; an outer fifo: 3 / 3 / 3; wall time unchanged (26-35 s). Not run on Linux, Windows or through the Rust `semio-build-scheduler`. Every compiling caller takes a pooled lane (test and lint runners, exact laws, wasm build, relay, wgpu, the component driver, every artifact build through `buildRepositoryCargoArtifacts`, the proctor and the Hub's Cargo checks), so none holds a whole-directory custody; a lane's provenance receipts are published into the shared ledger before the lane is released, and two artifact builds compile at the same time (measured: pair 12.5 s to 8.1 s, custody wait 6.2 s to 0.06 s, peak RSS 0.8 to 1.4 GB). A cold lane compiled officially in `print-rs:test` (gate 3b, 192 s). A test that needs a Rust oracle builds it through its own issued invocation, never a nested `repo cargo`.

- **A running dashboard never blocks a build.** Dashboard installs are content addressed (`dashboard-cli/<mode>/<digest>/semio`, `installed.json` swapped by rename) and a running dashboard holds only a SHARED lease on its digest directory. Cargo is reproducible, so a rebuild after a peer edit that does not change the binary (tests, docs, mtimes) yields the SAME digest; the publish used to request an EXCLUSIVE lease on it first and, behind the runner, failed after the 5-minute build with `Refusing to wait … dashboard-cli/prototype/<digest>`. Now an intact digest (marker owner, exact file list, single-link regular file, executable bit, sha256 equal to the directory name) is verified under a shared lease and reused without a copy or an exclusive request; a new digest is published beside the running one (the running inode is never replaced, on Windows a running .exe is never overwritten); only an absent or damaged digest takes the exclusive lease, as a try-lock, re-reading with bounded back-off, never hold-and-wait. After each install the current digest and the 2 newest are kept and older ones are reclaimed when nobody holds them (a held one is kept; before this nothing ever reclaimed them: 569 MB in 20 digests). Official-route proof still owed: hold the installed digest with `dashlock-g-tools/hold-installed.ts prototype`, touch a dashboard source file that leaves the binary equal, run `repo-dashboard-rs:build`, expect exit 0 and the same digest.

- **Cargo preparation without a child process** (live): every Rust test or build used to start a second `repo` process (`synchronize`: owner closure, member rosters, `cargo --version`) only to find its receipt `<key>.sync.json` still valid, 23-42 s of every `print-rs:test` action. The child now also writes `<key>.witness.json` next to the receipt (every answer its plans read: listings of sub-directories, `Cargo.toml` and lock texts, `.cargo/config.toml`, `CARGO_*` variables, and the stamp of the toolchain's `cargo`, each with a stat taken before the answer) and the caller replays it in the process in 0.3-0.4 s. The child starts only when an answer differs: a member or owner manifest, the lock, a new directory, the Cargo configuration, a `CARGO_*` variable, the toolchain, a deleted receipt or witness, or a recipe that has to run.
- **Lock order of the Rust path** (live): the compiler writer custody (`repo:compiler-writer:`, class `image`) is entered after the Cargo store, custody and recipe leases, and artifacts are published after it. Before, a queued exclusive writer could close a cycle between a native pass, a relay and the writer that only the cycle detector or 120 s resolved. A cold dashboard build prints no `[lease-order]` line with `repo:compiler-writer:` held.
- **Source admission of repository tests** (live): the foreign digest book and the admission memos that `bun repo cargo` used now serve the repository tests too, so the admission of the 375 units of a `print-rs:test` action is expected to take about 3 s on the second run instead of 45 s.

- **Dashboard no-op** (live, G41-G43): `repo-dashboard-rs:build` returns the installed binary without a nested `repo run … :compile` when Cargo's own source list holds. The compile records the dep-info of the binary (every source file the executable was compiled from, including `include_str!` files outside the crate roots) in `semio.sources.json` beside the executable, `installed.json` (version 4) keeps it with the build start, and a run checks it by stat only: the same captured sources (roots, manifests, `Cargo.lock`, the Cargo profile policy files of `🎚️mode/🦀️profile`), no recorded source changed (the later of mtime and ctime counts, so a replacement that keeps old timestamps counts), the executable in place with its recorded size and mode. It then prints `[dashboard] prototype is current: its … source files and the … files its executable was compiled from are unchanged since its build, so nothing is built`. `repo dashboard` uses the same rule (it used to miss `include_str!` and schema inputs). A change of the build tool's own code does not make a current installation stale; `repo run @semio-tech/repo-dashboard-rs:compile` and `:install` force a build. The plan of the nested compile is reusable from the second edit on (the per-action cancellation address `SEMIO_BAZEL_CONTROL_URL` is no plan input). Measured: no-op 10.0 s at 1.15 GB (was 140-357 s), the run after a landing 20-25 s, the first run after a library landing 95-470 s while 38-66 projects are re-inferred.

- **Incremental compiles of workspace crates** (live, G36-G39): a miss of the unit cache for a workspace crate that Cargo compiles incrementally keeps its rustc session in the lane and is still published (the key ignores the incremental directory; registry crates never get the flag); miss events carry the suffix ` incremental:lane`. A one-line edit of print's crate recompiles in 8 s instead of 46-76 s (the first compile in a lane costs about 3 % more and seeds the session). The idle-lane quota counts files only, not session bytes (those have their own 10 % share; `lane.size` records `incrementalBytes`), so another product's run no longer trims the lane that holds the session.
- **Start of a Rust test action** (live, G42-G43): a test or lint run looks the packages it names up in their workspace members instead of walking an inventory of 126,000 directories (15.6 s to milliseconds); the preparation witness keeps the entries of every listing, so a landing that only adds directories without a `Cargo.toml` leaves the receipt valid and starts no preparation child (15-30 s of the first print action after such a landing); the capture of the Cargo unit graph is served from a memo (at most 4 records per store, keyed by program, arguments, workspace, Cargo configuration, toolchain and the Cargo-read environment; its inputs are the manifests, the lock and the target discovery paths) in about 0.5 s while sources change but manifests do not; and Nextest gets the planned metadata as `--cargo-metadata <file>`, so it starts no `cargo metadata` of its own.

## 🧪️ Tests and routing tables

- Every build-library test file is routed to exactly one suite in `🏗️build/🗂️suites/🔣️.json` (51 suites, law `build-route-table`); the caching module has its own table (`⚡️caching/🗂️suites/🔣️.json`, 102 suites). A suite runs as `bun repo run @semio-tech/repo:test-<suite>` (targets in `⚡️caching/📋️project.json`). A new test file must be added to a suite or the route law fails. Process-module and product tests have their own tables.
- Census (route-g, 10-26): of 353 build-library files 270 were green in official mode; the 83 others are classed (52 stale expectations, 16 birth or Nx gated, 7 real defects, 5 peer, 2 load); fixes for them are pending gate (route2-g).
- **Go tests cannot touch the real checkout**: the sandbox guard of the Go test route (`🏠️workspace/📦️packages/🐹️go/🏖️sandbox`) compares roots by identity (same file, so links, case folding and Unicode normalisation agree): a test that resolves or binds the checkout root ITSELF aborts at the first resolution, naming the test, before it can write anything (the default; `SEMIO_SANDBOX_GUARD=report` lists all offenders, only for modules already fixed). A directory below the checkout with its own `.git` is a root of its own and passes (`BindRoot` and the fixture repository run a real `git init`, so git itself no longer answers for the enclosing checkout). The official route used to issue TMPDIR below the checkout, where a test directory without a `.git` resolved to the REAL checkout and wrote fixture tickets and sessions into it; `RelocateScratch` now moves such a scratch directory out (user cache directory, `/tmp`, `/var/tmp`, with no repository above it; `SEMIO_SANDBOX_SCRATCH=keep` opts out and `SEMIO_TEST_ARTIFACT_DIR` is untouched). 33 Go modules audited: 21 run under the sandbox, 8 never resolve a root, the launcher tests read the checkout read-only. Open: about 40 direct `SetRootDir(t.TempDir())` sites in the cli, hooks, move, tree, codebase and graphql tests rely on the relocation, not on a fence of their own; two graphql autofix tests are red independently of the guard.
- **Platform verdicts**: every suite of the three route tables (220: process 67, caching 102, build 51) has an explicit `platforms` verdict derived from evidence in its test files, with its reasons in `platformCauses` (six causes: `posix-program`, `posix-shell-script`, `python3-command`, `symlink`, `permission-bits`, `posix-process-control`; closed rules in `🗂️suites/🖥️platforms/🔣️.json`, an independent Python reading as the oracle). 147 suites are Windows-capable (process 54 of 67, caching 68 of 102, build 25 of 51), 73 are POSIX-only (darwin, linux). A POSIX-only suite on Windows prints `SKIPPED, not passed` (exit 0) and counts as skipped, never passed, and its project target is uncacheable (`live`) so a skip is never replayed as a pass. In `.github/workflows/build-system.yml` no row is advisory any more and the `suites` job has no `continue-on-error`: every Windows-capable suite blocks. After adding a suite or a platform-specific test re-derive the verdicts with `python3 -I .🧬semio/🦑️repo/🎫️tickets/🎆️26/🌙️10/☀️18/GET-CODEBASE-RUNNING-END-TO-END-AGAIN/xplat4-g-tools/staged/platforms/tag.py`. Eight suites that the CI policy already requires on Windows still carry unconditioned POSIX facts (`descriptor-lifetime`, `process-tree-termination`, `build-facets`, `native-storage`, `leases`, `cargo-unit-cache-processes`, `build-inputs-revision`, `build-bazel-execution`): the first Windows run decides whether they get gates or leave `mustRun`. The evidence is static; it cannot see path separators in expectations, CRLF, the 256-character limit or a missing tool.
- Graph-free single file: `nice -n 10 bun test ./<file>` from the module directory, always with `./` (a bare path is a filter: bun scans the whole directory, holds every folder open and runs nothing; `bun test ./` with nothing behind it names the checkout and is refused too). Laws that need the repo envelope run through their suite route. A test that opens cache storage runs from the checkout root: started in a module directory it is refused (English and German) instead of creating a second set of images there (that is how 16 GiB of images arose below `🧰️framework`). Ad-hoc scripts stream their input and stay below 500 MB.

## 🪟️ Windows, Linux and macOS

The same commands everywhere; where a platform differs the code says so and its law runs through injected platforms on any host. Nothing here has run on Windows: the statements are static or modelled, one real Windows CI run decides.

- **Renames over open files**: a publish rename over a file another process holds open fails on Windows (and under virus scanners). The four publish renames of the observation layer (`👁️observation`, `📥️inputs`, two each) now retry through `renameOverBusyFileSyncV1` with the layer's declared pauses and throw the last busy error; any other error is immediate.
- **Installer mutex**: the PowerShell installer takes `Global\` first (cross-session exclusion) and falls back to `Local\` when the account may not create it (`UnauthorizedAccessException`).
- **Symbolic links and the C library**: tests that need links run only where links can be made (one probe; a CI host must be able to), and the FFI probe picks `libSystem`, `ucrtbase` or `libc.so.6` per platform. The Node native core has a pure `win32` plan law (account name of 40 characters, both msvc targets, `.exe` compiler, 256-character limit refusal) and a real Windows law that builds the core, loads it under `node.exe` and checks all 7 primitives; it fails, not skips, on a Windows CI host without `rustc`.
- **Unknown free space is not healthy space**: a failing `statfs` used to count as plenty. The lease-store pressure sweep now treats an unmeasurable store as under pressure and prints `? % free` (EN and DE), and `cache dedupe` reports `clones:false` (a volume without clones replaces nothing) and says that the gain is unknown when the free space cannot be read.
- Not run on Windows: the real core law, the installer from a standard account, the rename retry against a real open handle.

## 🩹️ Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Meaning and action |
|---|---|
| `[capacity] waiting 42s for run: not enough free memory (need … MB; … spare above the … MB floor …)` | admission queue, not a hang; reasons: `memory`, `swap`, `pressure-*`, `files`, `attaches`, `load`, `ramp`, `queue-order`; read `bun repo capacity status`; Ctrl+C gives everything back. On a saturated host (load 30-65) a course build waited 46 min. |
| `[capacity] run: forecast quiet because the earlier run of its class finished 90s ago …` followed by `waiting …` | a hot no-op after a finished run of its class holds only the small quiet reservation (1500 to 2048 MB, not the 5632 to 7168 MB of a busy class) and passes the soft stops (raised memory pressure, load, open files, attached images); critical pressure, swap or a real memory shortage still hold it. A class whose last run ended more than 30 minutes ago is asked for the full grant again: run the build twice. To plan as a smaller machine on a bigger host: `SEMIO_HOST_CAPACITY_TOTAL_MEGABYTES=16384 SEMIO_HOST_CAPACITY_CORES=8` (it only shrinks; a malformed value means the real host). |
| `[capacity] run: reservation grown from 2048 MB to 4823 MB of the 8190 MB it asked for, after 0s: nothing else of ours is running and only that much memory is free (…); the run goes on` | not a hang: a quiet run (or any run that is the only one running) needed more than the host has free right now, so it is admitted at the largest size that fits and goes on with the jobs that memory carries (`… the run goes on with 4 of 8 job(s)`, never fewer than one), growing later when memory frees up ("Growing a reduced grant"); it never waits for memory on itself. With peers running the same run waits in the fair queue (`… waiting 12s to grow the reservation from … MB to … MB`) and grows when the memory is free. The size it asks for is bounded by what its own class measured (else its declared ceiling, else 8192 MB), never by 200% of the default. A wait of many minutes beside 10 GB of spare memory is a defect: report the class and `bun repo capacity status`. |
| `Waiting for access to repository-graph-facts:…:shard:7 … position 3 of 9, behind … holder: progressing, inference 120 of 566, about 34 min left` | a peer builds the graph; you are served by the coverage stamp when it publishes; for read-only queries use `SEMIO_BUILD_GRAPH_POLICY=current`; `bun repo leases status` shows STUCK/STALE holders |
| swap growing, machine sluggish | `bun repo capacity status` (swap, pressure, who holds what); do not run `bun`, `tsc` or Python scripts outside `repo capacity run`; stop what you started |
| An action does not see a variable of your shell (a missing token, a switch without effect, `command not found` for a tool outside the standard `PATH`) | by design: action processes get the allowlist (see "Action environment"), not the shell. Declare the variable as an `env` input of the task (explicit and reviewable), then run once with `--skip-cache` so the action really executes. |
| ENFILE, `Too many open files in system`, `kern.num_files` near 245,760 | the host file table is full. The 20:07 incident of 10-26 (122,880 descriptors = `kern.maxfilesperproc`, killing measured runs) was `bun test <path>` WITHOUT `./` (the 17:43 one has the same signature): bun reads such an operand as a FILTER, scans the whole checkout (465,000 files, 40 s, 2 GB), holds every directory open and runs no test. Find it with `ps` (a `bun test` with a bare path), stop that pid, never `pkill`; write `./path` or an absolute path. The owned runners now refuse it before any process starts (`[tests] bun test <operand> reads the operand as a filter, not as a path … Prefix it with ./`; also for `./` or `.` naming the checkout); `SEMIO_TEST_OPERAND_GUARD=off` lifts the guard. It cannot see a plain `bun test` typed in a shell or spawned by your own tool. Other ENFILE causes are transient and commands retry (`busy`). |
| exit 75, foreign mount, withheld project, launcher contract | see "When it goes wrong" |
| `bun repo doctor` | one-shot answer for waiting, slow or failing work (pins, images, leases, grants, generations); `--fix --dry-run` lists the safe repairs |
| `bun repo fleet status [--json]` | the whole picture in one read-only screen (about 0.4-0.5 s, 93 MB, no lease, no graph): a source that fails or hangs names itself and leaves out only its own section; a run started before the standing-record format shows `code unknown`; waiting-time estimates are printed only where earlier holds taught one |

## 📏️ Measuring a build

- **A no-op is the third run.** After a landing of build-tool code the first run re-keys the graph memo and replans Bazel (sometimes it re-infers projects), the second seals the memo, the third and every later one is hot. Run `bun repo run <project>:<target>` three or four times in a row and report the last ones. A real build also writes the product's own store (the course: `.🧾️store/build.json`), so the first no-op after an executing build can be refused once (graph memo `changed: …/.🧾️store/build.json`).
- **Measure from one clean environment.** Agent, desktop and session variables and `SEMIO_REPO_PROFILE` no longer re-key actions or end a plan, but every other variable the planner reads does (`planning again: e NAME changed`), and alternating between a shell with your variables and a script without them replans on every switch. Start measured runs from one fixed environment: the gate's `gate-g-tools/product.py` drops `CLAUDE*`, `ANTHROPIC*`, `__CF*`, `XPC_*`, `TERM_SESSION_ID` and `SEMIO_SCRIPT_STALL_MS`, sets `SEMIO_LANGUAGE=en`, runs at `nice -n 10`, ends its own process group above 20 GB or after 45 min of running time (time parked in a queue does not count) and records wall time, the peak resident memory of the whole process tree and the exit code; without it use `env -i` with `PATH`, `HOME` and `TMPDIR`.
- **Read the proof lines, not only the wall time.** A hot no-op prints `graph: … served from the memo …`, `[bazel] plan reused: … observations …` and Bazel's `INFO: 1 process: 1 internal` (nothing executed). `7 worker` there means actions re-executed: compare the `t_*.input` files of two runs in the generated Bazel workspace to find the one that moved. Host load changes absolute seconds by a factor of 2-4 (compare CPU time), waiting in the capacity queue is not part of the build, and a peer's landing between two runs is one replan.
- **`SEMIO_REPO_PROFILE=1|json`** prints the phase table of every process and is safe to leave on: its output goes to standard error, never keys an action and does not end a plan (it used to re-execute seven course actions on every run). One-line edit probe: append a line to a product source, run, restore the file byte for byte, run again; both runs must print `plan reused` and only the affected action executes.
- **Measured 10-27** (32 GB Mac, load 11-55, 16 GB budget simulated by policy; gate runs g34-g42): architecture course hot no-op 10.0-10.1 s at 0.9-1.3 GB (memo served, plan reused), one-line edit 25-50 s, revert 35-40 s; print hot no-op 10-15 s at 1.6 GB (550 projects served from the memo), one-line edit 85.5 s, revert 15 s (served from the memo), the first edit after a landing 161 s; dashboard no-op 10.0 s at 1.15 GB, 20-25 s the run after a landing. The first run after a library landing re-infers owners (dashboard 38 in 95 s, print 355 in 600 s at a 10.3 GB peak).

## 📍️ Status

Measured 10-26 and 10-27 (gate runs g23-g42) on a 32 GB / 10-core Mac with about 20 agents (load 11-65), 16 GB simulated by policy; absolute seconds are 2-4x an idle host, and `product.py` rounds wall times up to 5 s steps.

- Met: dashboard rebuild after a one-line edit under 2 min and under 3 GB; no-wait admission of at least 4 runs on 16 GB with learned sizes; queue time 2.3x lower in the replay; graph-free `cache status` tree 580 MB (was 2363 MB), Bazel worker 98 MB (was 1770 MB), a waiting run 35-75 MB; graph builder flat at 3.7-5.0 GB (was 15-29 GB); one-project graph change 34 s; environment move 144-159 s to 43-52 s; test-owner rediscovery 69 s to 3-5 s.
- Targets of wave 1026g, measured 10-27 on the latest gate logs (g34-g42; `product.py`, peak = resident memory of the whole process tree):

| Target | 10-26 | 10-27 and verdict |
|---|---|---|
| Course hot no-op under 10 s and 1.5 GB | 60-83 s | 10.0-10.1 s at 0.9-1.3 GB (memo served, plan reused): at target |
| Course one-line edit under 60 s | 506 s | 25-50 s, the revert 35-40 s: met |
| Rust (print) one-line edit under 2 min | 2,637 s | 85.5 s, the revert 15 s, served from the memo; the first edit after a landing 161 s: met |
| Print hot no-op | 420-560 s | 10-15 s at 1.6 GB, 550 projects served from the memo: met |
| Dashboard no-op | 140-240 s | 10.0 s at 1.15 GB, 20-25 s the run after a landing, 95-470 s the first after a library landing: met |
| Peer edits without re-execution | never measured | a library landing still re-infers 38-355 owners: not met |
| Graph after a peer edit under 2 min, builder under 2 GB | 307 s | one project 30-40 s; a 355-owner refresh peaks at 10.3 GB (14-16 GB before the fixed graph child heap): not met |
| Three agents on 16 GB | never measured | not run yet |
| Disk budget with lock-aware GC | none | budgets and sweeps landed (C1, memo 1 GiB), steady state not measured |

- Not met or not shown today: peer edits (a landing re-infers owners and the graph builder reaches 10.3 GB on 355), the three-agent scenario, a steady-state disk measurement, a hot run on a Windows or Linux host.
- Baseline of 10-26 (bench2-g, quiet window 18:54-19:36, before the evening batches; re-measure with `repo bench compare` on reports of the same host class): a hot course no-op took 60 / 77 / 83 s and none of it was Bazel (graph freshness 9-27 s, tool-setup 32-48 s, dependency verification 5-6 s, admission 3-7 s, capacity wait 0-19 s), a one-line course edit 506 s (graph 307 s for one project, tool-setup 85 s, execution 60 s), and the dashboard prime failed after 909 s with `Refusing to wait` behind a human's dashboard. The graph memo and plan reuse removed freshness and tool-setup from a hot run, the quiet grant the capacity wait.
- Pending gate: the graph closure classifier (backlog group D3), the Nx fixes (E1), fleet status (F1) and the sealed default (the flip, last); deferred: the managed-parent guard (store-g). Landed since the list of 10-26: cache-key narrowing, route fixes, index chunks and graph memory (D1), the platform and test tables (A1), hygiene (B1), disk budgets and Cargo job tokens (C1).
- In progress: cross-platform CI for the suite tables (ci-g).
- Not verified: Windows and Linux (the Windows lease lock and unit-cache wrapper are modelled and tested through injected platforms; one real Windows CI run is needed), a live yield between two graph builders.

